```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": ["Original request S1", "Original request S2", "Exercise context: empty repository, unattended intake, no tools or files"],
  "headline": "Intake established the sales-summary outcomes and recorded defaults for an unattended implementation.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "A person or script runs a Python command from a terminal or shell; the request does not identify a particular audience."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "For supplied sales records, standard output shows the correct total revenue, revenue grouped by region, and units grouped by product."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "Build a Python CLI that reads sales CSV files and prints the three requested summaries; S2 also requires tests. Other inputs, reports, interfaces, and deployment are outside the requested scope."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No existing repository behavior is identified; the exercise states that the repository is empty."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume `python sales_summary.py FILE [FILE ...]`, with UTF-8 CSV headers `region,product,units,unit_price`; revenue is units multiplied by unit price. Print `Total revenue: VALUE`, then `Revenue per region:` and `Units per product:` sections with `NAME: VALUE` lines sorted by name. Sum all supplied files together and use exact decimal arithmetic for revenue. Discovery should look for authoritative examples or conventions before these defaults are used."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume exit 0 on success and exit 2 with a descriptive message on standard error for missing arguments, unreadable files, malformed CSV, missing columns, or invalid records; print no partial summary on failure."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume each row is one sale, units are nonnegative integers, unit prices are finite nonnegative decimals, and region and product names are nonempty. Aggregate identical names exactly as supplied; a header-only file contributes zero. No date, currency conversion, filtering, or return-handling rule is supplied."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume missing required headers or values, empty region or product names, non-integer or negative units, non-finite or negative prices, and malformed CSV are invalid."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The process owns in-memory aggregate totals for the duration of one command invocation; source CSV files remain with their existing owners and no application state is persisted."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Anyone with access to the process output can see its summary, subject to the execution environment's permissions. Concurrent changes to input files have no consistency guarantee; a restart reads the files again and recomputes the summary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume Python 3.9 or newer; discovery should first check for an applicable workspace runtime convention."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume Python standard-library dependencies only; no third-party dependency is required."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume only readable CSV paths as command arguments; no port, environment variable, or configuration file is needed."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep CSV parsing and aggregation callable separately from argument handling and output so their behavior can be checked independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume standard-library unittest, run with `python -m unittest discover`."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Run the CLI against representative CSV files and check standard output and exit status for all three aggregates; also check multiple files, decimal arithmetic, invalid input, and failure output."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Deliver the source and tests in the run's source checkout for use from a Python command line. Execution requires a suitable Python runtime and read access to the supplied CSV files; no deployed target is named."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No numeric performance, security, accessibility, or presentation bounds are specified. Assume ordinary local CLI use and human-readable output without adding a separate interface."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The largest acceptance risk is assuming the wrong sales schema, revenue formula, or output contract; incorrect decimal arithmetic and incomplete error handling are additional risks."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Discovery of representative records or an authoritative contract would expose incompatible assumptions early; a small end-to-end example with independently calculated totals would expose calculation or output errors."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 — Outcome, fact: a Python CLI must read sales CSV files and print total revenue, revenue per region, and units per product; it becomes usable through the delivered source checkout and is visible in standard output when invoked after the final return. Boundary, fact: this is an unattended intake-only exercise with an empty repository, no tools or files available during intake, no implementation in this stage, and no named deployed target; an isolated run's source checkout stays at its snapshot until final return, and any deployed outcome would exist only after release. Assumptions: the CLI contract, schema, calculation rules, errors, runtime, and other defaults are recorded in the answers. Open questions: discovery should establish any authoritative CSV examples, revenue rules, output conventions, and workspace runtime conventions before implementation; these block finalizing the corresponding defaults. Source hints are leads rather than verified facts: any available team or workspace, channels, internal sites or documents, private repositories, owners, and the ambiguous terms sales record and revenue; none is identified by the request. Keep any sensitive locator in an authorized private store, never in committed KNOWLEDGE/. No person-only decision is required to proceed with the recorded defaults. Interaction and state assumptions: the actor is whoever invokes the CLI, identified only by the operating environment; there is no application-managed shared state across people, devices, or sessions, aggregates last for one invocation, process exit ends them, and the runtime supplies Python, filesystem access, and standard streams. Output is visible to those with access to it, with no additional application hiding; these defaults rule out accounts, shared sessions, persistent application state, and application-level access controls. S2 — Outcome, fact: add tests, including checks at the command boundary; they become available in the delivered source checkout after final return and are observable when run."
}
```
